darlingserver: remember a Thread::resume() that arrives before suspend() (pending-wakeup flag).

Root cause (established with the dsdiag counters of darlingserver-resume-trace.patch): resume() returned early
while !_suspended, so a wakeup that raced the target thread entering suspend() was dropped and the thread
(kernelAsync runner) parked forever with work queued. Fix: resume() on a running thread sets _pendingWakeup
(under _rwlock, the lock that also guards _suspended); suspend() consumes it and returns without parking
(releasing unlockMe); with a continuation, doWork() reschedules the thread when it is back at the top. Several
resumes collapse into the one flag. Idle, dead or terminating threads get no flag; a flag left over when a call
ends without suspending is cleared as stale.

This patch supersedes darlingserver-resume-trace.patch (same hunks, kept unlisted in series; the dsdiag counters
are unchanged, plus suspend_absorbed_wakeup / resume_flag_*). It also adds the selftest: with
DSERVER_RESUME_SELFTEST=1 the server runs 200 bursts of 64 kernelAsync lambdas and prints
RESUME-SELFTEST PASS / FAIL (20 s per round timeout) then exits; DSERVER_SELFTEST_SUSPEND_DELAY_US=N delays
every suspend() by N us to force the race.

Also (found with tests/src/suspend-wake-probe.c): a resume() that lands after suspend() set _suspended but before the
thread has switched back to the top of its microthread schedules the thread; the worker reaching doWork() sees
_running and used to drop the wakeup. doWork() now records it as _pendingWakeup for a suspended thread and the
doneWorking handler reschedules it.

Decision on darlingserver-thread-suspend-lock.patch (shift 2): KEPT, not a duplicate of this flag in effect.
It serialises thread_release()/dtape_thread_wait_while_user_suspended (duct-tape thread.c, user-suspension
path) with one global lock. The kernelAsync semaphore path never takes it, and the pre-suspend-lock server
(run old1) showed the same hang, so it was not the cause. For the user-suspension path the flag would also
cover the window, so the lock is now redundant there only; dropping it is untested and was not done.

DIAG resume logging (fix53): the scheduled line is rate limited per tid (first 32, then every 65536th with n=).
One tid resumed ~11000/s and filled the whole 100 MB debug tail (fix52: 751879 of 751880 lines); with the limit the
full server log fits from the start (fix53: 1525 DIAG lines of 350004). The full rate goes to a ring instead:
DSERVER_DIAG_RING=<entries> maps <prefix>/private/var/log/dserver-diag-ring.bin MAP_SHARED (survives any exit,
no flush), decoded by build-freebsd/diag-ring-decode.py.

